a}_d)$.\n        b. Draw a word $w_{d,i} \\sim \\text{Categorical}(\\vec{\\phi}_{z_{d,i}})$.\n\n#### **3. Mathematical Formalism**\n\n*   **Hyperparameters (Priors):**\n    *   $\\vec{\\alpha}$: The $K$-dimensional parameter of the Dirichlet prior on the per-document topic distributions. It controls the sparsity of topics within a document.\n    *   $\\vec{\\beta}$: The $V$-dimensional parameter of the Dirichlet prior on the per-topic word distributions. It controls the sparsity of words within a topic.\n\n*   **Latent Variables (Unobserved):**\n    *   $\\vec{\\theta}_d$: The $K$-dimensional topic distribution vector for document $d$.\n    *   $\\vec{\\phi}_k$: The $V$-dimensional word distribution vector for topic $k$.\n    *   $z_{d,i}$: The latent topic assignment for the $i$-th word in document $d$.\n\n*   **Observed Variable:**\n    *   $w_{d,i}$: The $i$-th word in document $d$, which is the only variable we actually observe.\n\n#### **4. The Goal of Inference and The Final Feature Vector**\n\nThe generative process describes how documents are created. In practice, our task is the inverse: given the observed documents (the words $W$), we want to infer the latent structure (the topic assignments $Z$, the topic distributions $\\Phi$, and the per-document topic proportions $\\Theta$). This is a Bayesian inference problem, aiming to compute the posterior distribution $P(\\Theta, \\Phi, Z \\mid W, \\vec{\\alpha}, \\vec{\\beta})$.\n\nSince this is computationally intractable, approximation methods like **Gibbs Sampling** or **Variational Inference** are used.\n\nAfter the model is trained, the key output for our classification task is the inferred per-document topic proportion vector, $\\vec{\\theta}_d$. This vector is our **LDA-Based Thematic Vector**.\n\n$$\n\\vec{\\theta}_d \u003d (\\theta_{d,1}, \\theta_{d,2}, \\dots, \\theta_{d,K}), \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\sum_{k\u003d1}^{K} \\theta_{d,k} \u003d 1\n$$\n\n*   Each component $\\theta_{d,k} \u003d P(z_k \\mid d)$ r